-Yeah.
-And because it was like

00:08:09.667 --> 00:08:11.167
four-hour naps,
I would go to work and, like,

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:13.934
go through the prosthetic, and
then shoot, and then come home.

00:08:13.934 --> 00:08:16.200
And even my daughter was asleep
or not, put her to bed,

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.734
and then wake up the next day.
You know, yeah.

00:08:17.734 --> 00:08:20.467
It was just, you know --
It's like this show, basically.

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:22.133
-Yeah. No, no.
You do much more work.

00:08:22.133 --> 00:08:23.667
-No, no, no.
But kind of, you know?

00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:25.334
Where it's like --
there's just the grind.

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:27.300
-Yeah. But you --
and you were loving it.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.501
-No, it was the -- it was the
most fun I've ever had, by far.

00:08:29.501 --> 00:08:30.901
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:08:30.901 --> 00:08:33.701
'Cause Leonard Bernstein --
it's about Leonard Bernstein

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:35.300
and his wife,
Felicia Montealegre,

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.834
who's played incredibly
by Carey Mulligan.

00:08:37.834 --> 00:08:39.234
-Yeah. She's great.
-She's amazing.

00:08:39.234 --> 00:08:40.667
Amazing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:40.667 --> 00:08:42.534
And, uh --
and Leonard Bernstein was --

00:08:42.534 --> 00:08:44.033
You know, he was --
he was punk rock.

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:46.000
So to be him was crazy.
It was amazing.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.534
-But you had the voice down,
by the way, bro.

00:08:47.534 --> 00:08:49.067
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Dude, you know, I would --

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.868
I wouldn't -- again,
I wouldn't bring it up.

00:08:50.868 --> 00:08:52.133
Uh, you were so good.

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:53.968
You had, like,
different layers of his voice.

00:08:53.968 --> 00:08:55.601
-Yeah. Because he had
a deviated septum.

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:58.367
He was asthmatic as a kid.
He smoked since he was 13.

00:08:58.367 --> 00:09:00.767
So his voice, you know,
slowly just got deeper

00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:02.734
and deeper and deeper.
-You crushed it.

00:09:02.734 --> 00:09:05.067
And is it hard to conduct?
-Impossible.

00:09:05.067 --> 00:09:06.267
Do you know about it?
I mean --

00:09:06.267 --> 00:09:08.133
I know a li-- I know --
-It's impossible.

00:09:08.133 --> 00:09:10.367
-I know that my kids have
a conductor in their thing,

00:09:10.367 --> 00:09:11.868
and they -- so they look
and they go --

00:09:11.868 --> 00:09:12.968
So if they're doing that...

00:09:12.968 --> 00:09:14.367
So we just went to go see
a Broadway show,

00:09:14.367 --> 00:09:16.000
and I'm like, "That's
the conductor right there,"

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.534
because that's who everyone
looks at the conductor like,

00:09:17.534 --> 00:09:18.734
"Oh, do this and that."

00:09:18.734 --> 00:09:21.033
So I mean --
But this does something, right?

00:09:21.033 --> 00:09:22.601
-Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:09:22.601 --> 00:09:24.501
-But that's something.
-You're keeping time.

00:09:24.501 --> 00:09:25.767
You're just letting
everybody know the tempo.

00:09:25.767 --> 00:09:27.234
The tempo changes.
And at the same time,

00:09:27.234 --> 00:09:30.000
you're making sure that
the decisions that you've made

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.501
to accentuate certain things
and bowing --

00:09:31.501 --> 00:09:34.868
I mean, it's impossible.
Quest, I mean, it's nuts.

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:37.334
-Yeah.
-It's so hard. It's crazy.

00:09:37.334 --> 00:09:38.868
So I spent like six years
just learning those

00:09:38.868 --> 00:09:41.334
6 1/2 minutes of Mahler's
Second Symphony,

00:09:41.334 --> 00:09:43.267
"The Resurrection,"
just so I could,

00:09:43.267 --> 00:09:45.434
you know, just try to do it.

00:09:45.434 --> 00:09:48.400
But it's impossible, conducting.
-You crush it, bud.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.968
I'm so happy for you.
I want to show a clip.

00:09:50.968 --> 00:09:54.734
Here is Bradley Cooper
in "Maestro." Take a look.

00:09:54.734 --> 00:09:57.033
[ Tchaikovsky's
"The 1812 Overture" plays ]

00:09:57.033 --> 00:09:59.300
[ Laughter ]

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:09.167
♪♪

00:10:09.167 --> 00:10:10.300
[ Laughter ]

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.234
It's like...
"Remember that?"

00:10:12.234 --> 00:10:14.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.667
[ Both laugh ]

00:10:17.667 --> 00:10:21.000
It's like --
[ Laughter ]

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.100
It's like -- It's like --

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.501
It's like looking
at a different person,

00:10:23.501 --> 00:10:24.601
